    <dl class="faq">
      <dt>What is an NVOCC?</dt>
      <dd>A non-vessel-operating common carrier arranges ocean carriage and, where authorised under applicable rules, may issue its own bill of lading while using capacity provided by operating ocean carriers. Registration and obligations vary by jurisdiction.</dd>

      <dt>What is the difference between FCL and LCL?</dt>
      <dd>FCL normally involves a container allocated to a single shipment booking; LCL consolidates smaller shipments with other cargo. Cost, handling and transit processes can differ.</dd>

      <dt>What information is needed for a freight rate?</dt>
      <dd>Origin, destination, cargo description, packed dimensions or container requirement, gross weight, ready date, special handling instructions and the delivery terms are a useful starting point.</dd>

      <dt>What does a reefer container do?</dt>
      <dd>A refrigerated container uses integrated equipment to maintain specified transport conditions within its operating limits. It does not replace appropriate preparation or pre-cooling of cargo.</dd>

      <dt>What are Incoterms?</dt>
      <dd>Incoterms are internationally recognised trade terms published by the International Chamber of Commerce that define certain seller and buyer responsibilities. They do not replace the entire sales contract or the need for clear logistics instructions.</dd>

      <dt>Can Yentrans arrange door delivery?</dt>
      <dd>The possible delivery arrangement depends on the cargo, route, local service partners, licences and customs requirements. Please share your destination and desired delivery scope with our team for a shipment-specific assessment.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="disclaimer">These explanations are general information, not legal or regulatory advice, and
      are not specific to any country or date. Confirm requirements for your own route and shipment.</p>
